WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.067 --> 00:00:01.968
Our first guest tonight
is an Emmy,

00:00:01.968 --> 00:00:03.603
a Grammy and Tony-winning
actress,

00:00:03.603 --> 00:00:05.504
you know from shows such
as "Sex and the City,"

00:00:05.504 --> 00:00:08.708
"Ratched," and the new
HBO series, "The Gilded Age."

00:00:08.708 --> 00:00:11.176
She executive produces
and stars in

00:00:11.176 --> 00:00:12.511
"And Just Like That,"

00:00:12.511 --> 00:00:15.848
with new episodes premiering
Thursdays on HBO Max.

00:00:15.848 --> 00:00:17.216
Let's take a look.

00:00:17.216 --> 00:00:18.818
-I have a favor to ask,

00:00:18.818 --> 00:00:22.288
and it involves manual labor
and you two coming to Brooklyn.

00:00:22.288 --> 00:00:24.991
-Well, how can we say no?
-Aww.

00:00:24.991 --> 00:00:28.194
-Oh, no, no. I'm asking,
how can we say no?

00:00:28.194 --> 00:00:29.662
-You can't.

00:00:29.662 --> 00:00:32.064
We need a huge turnout
on Saturday to finish painting

00:00:32.064 --> 00:00:34.133
the women's shelter
I've been working on with Nya,

00:00:34.133 --> 00:00:36.569
and it would be so great
if you guys would come help.

00:00:36.569 --> 00:00:38.804
-I'm there,
and I'll bring the kids.

00:00:38.804 --> 00:00:40.239
I'm a really good painter.

00:00:40.239 --> 00:00:41.674
-Carrie?

00:00:41.674 --> 00:00:43.009
-Oh, well,

00:00:43.009 --> 00:00:44.710
when I was on stage crew
in high school,

00:00:44.710 --> 00:00:46.546
they asked me,
"Please stop painting,"

00:00:46.546 --> 00:00:48.948
so I will happily write a check.

00:00:48.948 --> 00:00:51.250
-You can't be the white lady
who just writes a check.

00:00:51.250 --> 00:00:53.419
If I can paint, you can paint.

00:00:53.419 --> 00:00:54.586
-Please welcome back
to the show,

00:00:54.586 --> 00:00:56.622
Cynthia Nixon, everybody.

00:00:56.622 --> 00:00:59.091
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:59.091 --> 00:01:05.064 line:0%
♪♪

00:01:05.064 --> 00:01:11.037 line:0%
♪♪

00:01:11.037 --> 00:01:17.009 line:0%
♪♪

00:01:17.009 --> 00:01:18.644
-I am so happy to
have you back here!

00:01:18.644 --> 00:01:20.279
-I am so happy to be here!

00:01:20.279 --> 00:01:22.081
-In a studio full of people,
no less.

00:01:22.081 --> 00:01:23.949
-So lovely.
-It's not bad, right?

00:01:23.949 --> 00:01:26.752
-And a band, even.
-And a band.

00:01:26.752 --> 00:01:28.755
We did see each
other right before --

00:01:28.755 --> 00:01:34.126
we were talking backstage --
right before the pandemic.

00:01:34.126 --> 00:01:35.728
Late February of 2020.
-Yes.

00:01:35.728 --> 00:01:38.264
-We were at the
Pen Awards in New York City,

00:01:38.264 --> 00:01:41.501
and it was a lovely evening
and a few people

00:01:41.501 --> 00:01:44.103
were starting to do fist pumps,
and I was like,

00:01:44.103 --> 00:01:45.938
"Come on, guys."

00:01:45.938 --> 00:01:47.507
And yet here we are,
a few years later.

00:01:47.507 --> 00:01:49.342
-Yeah, some people
are ahead of the curve.

00:01:49.342 --> 00:01:51.277
Not me, but yeah.
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:01:51.277 --> 00:01:53.946
Well, Christine Baranski and I
were there together,

00:01:53.946 --> 00:01:55.681
presenting an award
to Tom Stoppard.

00:01:55.681 --> 00:01:57.883
-And you and Christine Baranski

00:01:57.883 --> 00:02:00.854
are now playing sisters
in "The Gilded Age,"

00:02:00.854 --> 00:02:02.554
which is on HBO,
which is very exciting.

00:02:02.554 --> 00:02:04.990
But when you were first
on Broadway together,

00:02:04.990 --> 00:02:06.058
she was your mother.

00:02:06.058 --> 00:02:09.094
-Yes, in 1983,

00:02:09.094 --> 00:02:12.431
which I can't believe
is almost 40 years ago,

00:02:12.431 --> 00:02:15.334
she was playing my mother,
and I didn't realize

00:02:15.334 --> 00:02:16.836
she was really too young
to be my mother.

00:02:16.836 --> 00:02:19.806
But now we're sisters.
That's good. That's good.

00:02:19.806 --> 00:02:21.207
-You know,
right after, I feel like,

00:02:21.207 --> 00:02:22.342
maybe the last time we talked,

00:02:22.342 --> 00:02:24.677
I saw another
very young Cynthia Nixon.

00:02:24.677 --> 00:02:26.312
I had never seen
the movie "Amadeus."

00:02:26.312 --> 00:02:27.346
-Oh, yeah.

00:02:27.346 --> 00:02:28.848
-And you're in "Amadeus."
-Yes.

00:02:28.848 --> 00:02:31.384
-You just show up as a maid.
-Yes. Yes.

00:02:31.384 --> 00:02:33.419
-That must have been
one of your first film roles.

00:02:33.419 --> 00:02:35.954
-Oh, I had been acting about
five years at that point.

00:02:35.954 --> 00:02:38.224
-Well, I'm sorry that I've
missed the earlier work.

00:02:38.224 --> 00:02:40.359
-"Little Darlings"? You haven't
seen "Little Darlings?"

00:02:40.359 --> 00:02:41.493
-I'll put it on the list.

00:02:41.493 --> 00:02:43.429
-Well, but it was such
a big deal for me,

00:02:43.429 --> 00:02:45.598
because me and my friends,

00:02:45.598 --> 00:02:47.133
Milos Forman,
who directed that,

00:02:47.133 --> 00:02:48.901
was our absolute
favorite director

00:02:48.901 --> 00:02:50.836
from "Ragtime" and from "Hair."

00:02:50.836 --> 00:02:53.739
And I was in 11th grade,

00:02:53.739 --> 00:02:56.175
and I had already been
around the block a little bit.

00:02:56.175 --> 00:02:58.745
I kind of knew it was
five months in Czechoslovakia.

00:02:58.745 --> 00:03:00.613
I had never been to Europe,

00:03:00.613 --> 00:03:04.483
but I said after like, 6,000
auditions and they cast me,

00:03:04.483 --> 00:03:06.885
I said,
"I really want to do this part,

00:03:06.885 --> 00:03:11.124
but I'm in high school,
and could you, respectfully,

00:03:11.124 --> 00:03:12.959
if you don't use me
for two days in a row,

00:03:12.959 --> 00:03:15.027
could you fly me back
to New York?"

00:03:15.027 --> 00:03:16.228
And they did.

00:03:16.228 --> 00:03:17.863
-So you went back and forth
the whole time?

00:03:17.863 --> 00:03:20.766
-Yes. Yes.
Because I knew, even --

00:03:20.766 --> 00:03:23.803
I'd been around a little bit,
since I was 11, 12,

00:03:23.803 --> 00:03:25.772
and I knew they were just
going to put me in a hotel

00:03:25.772 --> 00:03:27.507
for months on end,

00:03:27.507 --> 00:03:29.375
and it was going to be
challenging in 11th grade.

00:03:29.375 --> 00:03:31.210
-Yeah.
-So they tested me.

00:03:31.210 --> 00:03:33.912
First, they flew me out
for a 45 minute rehearsal.

00:03:33.912 --> 00:03:36.382
-Really?
-To see if I balked.

00:03:36.382 --> 00:03:39.218
And I just, you know,
just smiled and smiled,

00:03:39.218 --> 00:03:41.754
and they flew me back and forth
like, five or six times.

00:03:41.754 --> 00:03:43.990
-You must have gotten
so many miles.

00:03:43.990 --> 00:03:45.224
-I did.

00:03:45.224 --> 00:03:46.492
-Also, I'm glad you said this.

00:03:46.492 --> 00:03:48.594
Please don't take this
the wrong way.

00:03:48.594 --> 00:03:51.197
When you said you were going
to be there for five months,

00:03:51.197 --> 00:03:52.932
you're not in
a lot of the movie.

00:03:52.932 --> 00:03:56.135
-No, that's what I mean. Right.
-Yeah.

00:03:56.135 --> 00:03:59.105
-But I know, I was very,
very low on the pecking order,

00:03:59.105 --> 00:04:00.873
and they're just going to
throw me in a hotel room

00:04:00.873 --> 00:04:02.775
and forget about me
and a few months later, be like,

00:04:02.775 --> 00:04:05.143
"Let's get that girl out here
for that one thing.

00:04:05.143 --> 00:04:07.146
She's got that one line."

00:04:07.146 --> 00:04:10.183
-Now the other thing about the
last time you were on this show,

00:04:10.183 --> 00:04:15.154
you were running for governor of
this state and you didn't win,

00:04:15.154 --> 00:04:19.892
but the guy who did win,
things didn't go great for.

00:04:19.892 --> 00:04:21.093
-And just like that,

00:04:21.093 --> 00:04:23.129
Andrew Cuomo was out of a job.

00:04:23.129 --> 00:04:27.065
-And just like that.
What a lovely tie-in.

00:04:27.065 --> 00:04:28.468
What a lovely tie-in.

00:04:30.903 --> 00:04:33.406
-You know, the difference
between him and me is,

00:04:33.406 --> 00:04:35.775
neither of us are governor.

00:04:35.775 --> 00:04:36.909
-Yeah.

00:04:36.909 --> 00:04:40.346
-But I've still got
to keep my Emmys.

00:04:40.346 --> 00:04:43.315
-That's true. He didn't even
get to keep his one Emmy.

00:04:43.315 --> 00:04:45.317
So congratulations
on that as well.

00:04:45.317 --> 00:04:47.320
So "And Just Like That,"

00:04:47.320 --> 00:04:50.456
I imagine all of you
must have been somewhat hesitant

00:04:50.456 --> 00:04:52.592
to return
to this beloved franchise.

00:04:52.592 --> 00:04:55.495
I think we were hesitant
and also very excited.

00:04:55.495 --> 00:04:58.731
Kind of equal measure because,
of course, we love the show.

00:04:58.731 --> 00:05:02.568
We love each other. We love
our characters, all that.

00:05:02.568 --> 00:05:05.805
But again, to revisit this show

00:05:05.805 --> 00:05:07.906
that everybody loves
so much, so iconic --

00:05:07.906 --> 00:05:11.844
and also,
I guess my main concern

00:05:11.844 --> 00:05:14.914
that I kept stating
over and over again was,

00:05:14.914 --> 00:05:17.083
if we go back,
it can't be a reboot.

00:05:17.083 --> 00:05:22.254
It can't just be this same show
almost 25 years later.

00:05:22.254 --> 00:05:24.490
We're very different people.
The world is different.

00:05:24.490 --> 00:05:26.759
It was always
an unbelievably white show,

00:05:26.759 --> 00:05:28.927
which was not so cool back then,

00:05:28.927 --> 00:05:33.299
and it's really, we obviously,
definitely have to change that.

00:05:33.299 --> 00:05:37.169
So it was also a chance
to hopefully, you know,

00:05:37.169 --> 00:05:38.938
we got a lot right
in that first show,

00:05:38.938 --> 00:05:40.239
but we got some things wrong

00:05:40.239 --> 00:05:42.574
and we got a chance
to go back and change them.

00:05:42.574 --> 00:05:44.810
-You did something that
I feel like a lot of people

00:05:44.810 --> 00:05:46.144
have done in recent years,

00:05:46.144 --> 00:05:48.080
which is, you went back
and binged the show itself.

00:05:48.080 --> 00:05:49.348
-Yes. Yes.

00:05:49.348 --> 00:05:52.584
-So were you watching it
as it went through?

00:05:52.584 --> 00:05:54.687
Would you watch each week
when you were making the show?

00:05:54.687 --> 00:05:56.088
-Oh, definitely.

00:05:56.088 --> 00:05:58.391
-But had you never watched it
all at once like that?

00:05:58.391 --> 00:05:59.992
-No. No.
-Yeah?

00:05:59.992 --> 00:06:02.228
-I mean, it was delightful.

00:06:02.228 --> 00:06:03.496
-I mean, I did it.

00:06:03.496 --> 00:06:04.864
We've talked about it,
but I did it with my wife.

00:06:04.864 --> 00:06:05.897
I'd never seen it.

00:06:05.897 --> 00:06:07.499
We binged it together,

00:06:07.499 --> 00:06:08.934
and I fell in love
with you ladies.

00:06:08.934 --> 00:06:10.570
I'm going to just be honest.
-Aww.

00:06:11.671 --> 00:06:13.339
[ Applause ]

00:06:13.339 --> 00:06:16.509
-Well, I think that, you know,
we were one of those things

00:06:16.509 --> 00:06:19.011
that people turned to
during the quarantine

00:06:19.011 --> 00:06:20.479
and really binged, you know?

00:06:20.479 --> 00:06:21.613
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:06:21.613 --> 00:06:23.049
-Well, I think that any...

00:06:23.049 --> 00:06:24.784
It was funny
the shows people found,

00:06:24.784 --> 00:06:27.419
because it was obviously
a deeply lonely time.

00:06:27.419 --> 00:06:29.589
And this was, you know,
this show has always been

00:06:29.589 --> 00:06:31.490
about friendship and connection,

00:06:31.490 --> 00:06:33.759
and so it makes total sense
to me that it would be

00:06:33.759 --> 00:06:35.895
a show that people
would sort of find comfort in

00:06:35.895 --> 00:06:37.429
during those difficult times.
-Yeah. Yeah.

00:06:37.429 --> 00:06:41.333
And, you know, as we were
all locked in our houses too,

00:06:41.333 --> 00:06:43.469
it's like, you know,
a bunch of people

00:06:43.469 --> 00:06:46.438
strutting around the city,
going to fabulous restaurants

00:06:46.438 --> 00:06:49.374
and wearing beautiful clothes
and doing all the things.

00:06:49.374 --> 00:06:52.378
-And it is such a snapshot
of that era of New York.

00:06:52.378 --> 00:06:54.447
It is.
-Wild. Wild. Yes.

00:06:54.447 --> 00:06:57.149
Matthew Broderick
always says this thing

00:06:57.149 --> 00:06:58.951
when he's walking down
the street with Sarah Jessica

00:06:58.951 --> 00:07:00.419
and he sees a girl
in a particular

00:07:00.419 --> 00:07:02.254
kind of an outfit and heels,
and he always says,

00:07:02.254 --> 00:07:04.323
"You did that.

00:07:04.323 --> 00:07:06.692
That's your fault."

00:07:06.692 --> 00:07:09.195
-That is very true. To this day,
that is very true.

00:07:09.195 --> 00:07:11.230
One thing
that you've also changed

00:07:11.230 --> 00:07:14.000
about this second iteration
of the show --

00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:15.334
the subway.

00:07:15.334 --> 00:07:16.669
We did not see much
of the subway

00:07:16.669 --> 00:07:18.070
in the first "Sex and the City."

00:07:18.070 --> 00:07:20.072
Maybe never even once.

00:07:20.072 --> 00:07:23.475
-Yeah, I think there was,
Carrie for about 30 seconds,

00:07:23.475 --> 00:07:26.045
briefly flirted
with taking a bus,

00:07:26.045 --> 00:07:28.046
and then someone said to her,
"You're on the bus.

00:07:28.046 --> 00:07:30.549
Why do you have to take the bus
if your photo is on the bus?"

00:07:30.549 --> 00:07:32.851
And she was like, "Yeah."

00:07:32.851 --> 00:07:37.757
Yeah, I mean, I certainly,
in non-COVID times,

00:07:37.757 --> 00:07:39.091
I'm on the subway every day.

00:07:39.091 --> 00:07:41.561
In COVID times I'm on
the subway maybe once a week.

00:07:41.561 --> 00:07:42.828
-Yeah.

00:07:42.828 --> 00:07:45.197
-And yeah, I mean,
I always felt like Miranda --

00:07:45.197 --> 00:07:46.832
you know,
Miranda works in Manhattan.

00:07:46.832 --> 00:07:48.634
She lives in Brooklyn.

00:07:48.634 --> 00:07:50.035
-She was probably on the subway.

00:07:50.035 --> 00:07:51.937
-I think she was on the subway,
but we never showed it.

00:07:51.937 --> 00:07:53.071
-We just never showed it.
-Yeah.

00:07:53.071 --> 00:07:54.273
-It's a different era now.

00:07:54.273 --> 00:07:56.208
People people deeply
want to see the subway.

00:07:56.208 --> 00:07:57.509
-They want to see the subway.

00:07:57.509 --> 00:07:59.244
-Because, let me say something
about New York City,

00:07:59.244 --> 00:08:00.646
if you've never been here,

00:08:00.646 --> 00:08:02.815
the subway is better
than it's ever been.

00:08:03.416 --> 00:08:05.584
[ Laughter ]

00:08:05.584 --> 00:08:08.854
And now you have two shows
on at the same time,

00:08:08.854 --> 00:08:10.622
and they both take place
in New York

00:08:10.622 --> 00:08:13.425
with a 140-year time gap.
-Yes.

00:08:13.425 --> 00:08:16.629
-So "The Gilded Age" is
late 1800s New York City.

00:08:16.629 --> 00:08:19.932
Do you enjoy the costume-drama-y
type of show?

00:08:19.932 --> 00:08:21.000
-I do.

00:08:21.000 --> 00:08:22.568
I mean, it's, you know,

00:08:22.568 --> 00:08:26.004
the corsets are not really
your friend.

00:08:26.004 --> 00:08:28.707
They're your adversary.
-Sure.

00:08:28.707 --> 00:08:31.910
-But certainly, the clothes are
amazingly beautiful and the wigs

00:08:31.910 --> 00:08:34.513
and all of the sets.

00:08:34.513 --> 00:08:37.282
And then we shot in a lot of
real historic mansions

00:08:37.282 --> 00:08:39.952
in Troy, New York
and in Newport, Rhode Island.

00:08:39.952 --> 00:08:41.687
And I mean,
it's gorgeous to look at,

00:08:41.687 --> 00:08:44.690
and it's a period
not unlike our own

00:08:44.690 --> 00:08:48.794
where you have these --

00:08:48.794 --> 00:08:51.130
then, they were millionaires
who appeared out of nowhere

00:08:51.130 --> 00:08:52.765
because of
the Industrial Revolution

00:08:52.765 --> 00:08:55.401
and the railroads
and business was booming.

00:08:55.401 --> 00:08:58.036
And, you know, they built
these palatial mansions.

00:08:58.036 --> 00:09:01.073
Now we have these billionaires
who appear out of nowhere

00:09:01.073 --> 00:09:03.542
because of the tech boom
and they're, you know,

00:09:03.542 --> 00:09:05.243
visiting outer space and stuff.

00:09:05.243 --> 00:09:08.280
So it's similar, kind of like,
people with so much money

00:09:08.280 --> 00:09:09.849
they really just don't know
what to do with it.

00:09:09.849 --> 00:09:11.050
-They don't know
what to do with it.

00:09:11.050 --> 00:09:12.351
At least 140 years ago,

00:09:12.351 --> 00:09:14.119
they built houses
that are still here.

00:09:14.119 --> 00:09:17.589
I don't know what this
space thing is gonna give us.

00:09:17.589 --> 00:09:20.359
I have one thing I got to say.

00:09:20.359 --> 00:09:21.661
We've talked about
Steve in the past.

00:09:21.661 --> 00:09:23.762
-Yeah.

00:09:23.762 --> 00:09:26.231
-Steve's not having
a great time in the new show.

00:09:26.231 --> 00:09:27.432
-He is not.
-Yeah.

00:09:27.432 --> 00:09:29.234
You know I love Steve.

00:09:29.234 --> 00:09:30.669
-I love Steve, too.

00:09:30.669 --> 00:09:33.039
-Feel like I don't know why you
guys did him like that.

00:09:33.039 --> 00:09:35.040
-Awww.

00:09:35.040 --> 00:09:36.475
-And part of it is, you know,

00:09:36.475 --> 00:09:40.045
that I have a very good
Steve impression.

00:09:40.045 --> 00:09:41.513
-Would you like to
share it with me?

00:09:41.513 --> 00:09:42.815
-I would like to.

00:09:42.815 --> 00:09:44.216
-Would you like to share it
with all of us?

00:09:44.216 --> 00:09:45.884
-Well, I'd just like
to share it with you,

00:09:45.884 --> 00:09:47.820
but if they want to watch...
-Okay.

00:09:48.854 --> 00:09:50.756
[ Applause ]

00:09:53.392 --> 00:09:55.760
-[ As Steve ] Oh, Miranda.

00:09:55.760 --> 00:09:58.363
-Oh, that's very good.

00:09:58.363 --> 00:09:59.498
It's very good.

00:09:59.498 --> 00:10:02.067
-You're a very good actor.

00:10:02.067 --> 00:10:03.536
-If I was blindfolded,

00:10:03.536 --> 00:10:06.171
I would never know
it was not David.

00:10:06.171 --> 00:10:08.774
-You guys,
that is Cynthia Nixon.

00:10:08.774 --> 00:10:11.911
"And Just Like That"
and "The Gilded Age"

00:10:11.911 --> 00:10:13.412
are both available now
on HBO and HBO Max.

